From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nate Isern <nate@oxy.so>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Correct rand_chacha's allow_unsafe, which gnrt guesses wrong

`gnrt vendor` writes the `allow_unsafe` value for every crate it pulls in,
and for `rand_chacha` it writes `false` while the crate does use `unsafe`.
Both vendored epochs then fail to compile:

    error: usage of an `unsafe` block
       --> ../../third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/vendor/rand_chacha-v0_2/src/chacha.rs:86:45
    error: aborting due to 3 previous errors

    error: usage of an `unsafe` block
       --> ../../third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/vendor/rand_chacha-v0_3/src/chacha.rs:91:45
    error: aborting due to 3 previous errors

WHY GNRT GETS IT WRONG, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A GNRT BUG

`fill_allow_unsafe_settings` (tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs) asks
`unsafe_code_detector::contains_unsafe_code` per file. That detector parses
with `syn` and walks the AST for `ExprUnsafe` and `unsafe impl`. It never
descends into `syn::Macro::tokens`, and rand_chacha's only `unsafe` block
lives inside the body of `macro_rules! chacha_impl`, so nothing is found.

The detector's own doc comment names this exact miss, gives two upstream
crates it happens to, and says what to do about it:

    /// - Undetected `unsafe`:
    ///     - Will be auto-detected later, when `allow_unsafe = false` results
    ///       in a build error. [...]
    ///     - Examples (IIUC in both cases `unsafe` is hidden inside
    ///       `syn::Macro::tokens`) [...]

So the build failure IS the detection step, and correcting the value in
`gnrt_config.toml` is the prescribed response, not a workaround. The
`unwrap_or(false)` in `fill_allow_unsafe_settings` is deliberate for the same
reason: a wrong `false` announces itself, a wrong `true` does not.

THE AUDIT, BECAUSE `true` IS A CLAIM ABOUT SOURCE

Neither crate is a small amount of unsafe; it is one block, and the two
epochs are byte-identical in it. `grep -rnE '\bunsafe\b'` across the whole of
`rand_chacha-v0_2/` and `rand_chacha-v0_3/` returns exactly one line each:

    rand_chacha-v0_2/src/chacha.rs:86
    rand_chacha-v0_3/src/chacha.rs:91

both reading

    fn generate(&mut self, r: &mut Self::Results) {
        // Fill slice of words by writing to equivalent slice of bytes, then fixing endianness.
        self.state.refill4($rounds, unsafe {
            &mut *(&mut *r as *mut Array64<u32> as *mut [u8; 256])
        });
        for x in r.as_mut() {
            *x = x.to_le();
        }
    }

with `Self::Results = Array64<u32>` and `pub struct Array64<T>([T; 64]);`.
The block reinterprets an exclusive `&mut [u32; 64]` as `&mut [u8; 256]` so
the ChaCha core can write raw bytes, and the loop after it fixes endianness.

  * Size: 64 x 4 = 256, so the two views cover exactly the same bytes.
  * Alignment: the cast lowers the required alignment from 4 to 1, which is
    always sound in that direction.
  * Aliasing: `r` is an exclusive reference, the raw pointer is derived from
    it and immediately reborrowed with the same lifetime. No second reference
    to the buffer is live, and no user code runs in between.
  * Validity: every bit pattern is a valid `u32`, so writing arbitrary bytes
    through the `u8` view cannot produce an invalid value.

The one thing it leans on that Rust does not formally guarantee is that a
single-field tuple struct is laid out as its field. That is true of every
Rust compiler, and it is upstream's code unchanged since 2019 in the crate
the whole `rand` ecosystem uses; it is recorded here rather than glossed.

No transmutes, no pointer arithmetic, no unchecked indexing, no FFI, no
`unsafe impl Send`/`Sync`, no `unsafe fn`. The actual SIMD lives in
`ppv-lite86`, a separate crate that upstream already classifies
`allow_unsafe = true`.

WHERE THE CRATE IS ACTUALLY USED, WHICH BOUNDS WHAT THE VALUE COSTS

Neither epoch reaches the browser. Both are host build tooling, reached only
through the two `phf` code generators:

    selectors 0.24 --(build-dep)--> phf_codegen 0.8 --> phf_generator 0.8
                                 --> rand 0.7.3 --> rand_chacha 0.2.2
    cssparser 0.29 --> phf 0.10 --> phf_macros 0.10 --> phf_generator 0.10
                                 --> rand 0.8.7 --> rand_chacha 0.3.1

Confirmed by where the failures land: both are under
`clang_x64_for_rust_host_build_tools/`, the host toolchain, and neither
appears in the target-side graph.

WHY THE EDIT IS NOT EPOCH-SCOPED

It does not need to be. Unlike `thiserror`, whose two epochs genuinely
disagree, both rand_chacha epochs carry the same single block and both fail
the build without this line, so the unversioned key states one fact about
both and there is no collateral. Re-vendoring with this line changes
`allow_unsafe` in exactly two generated files,
`third_party/rust/rand_chacha/v0_2/BUILD.gn` and `.../v0_3/BUILD.gn`, and
nothing else anywhere under `third_party/rust`.

WHERE THIS SITS IN THE PIPELINE

Before vendoring, as with 059. `fill_allow_unsafe_settings` fills the value
through `entry("allow_unsafe").or_insert_with(...)`, so it writes only where the
key is ABSENT: declaring it here means the vendoring run finds it present and
leaves it alone rather than overwriting it with the wrong guess.

MEASURED

All three of 064, 065 and 066 applied, `tools/vendor-adblock-rust.sh` re-run,
then from chromium/src with the committed gn_args/linux.gn (verified
byte-identical to out/GateCheck/args.gn):

    $ ./buildtools/linux64/gn gen out/GateCheck
    Done. Made 29957 targets from 4411 files in 9591ms

    $ ./third_party/ninja/ninja -C out/GateCheck -k 0 \
          clang_x64_for_rust_host_build_tools/obj/third_party/rust/rand_chacha/v0_2/lib/librand_chacha_lib.rlib \
          clang_x64_for_rust_host_build_tools/obj/third_party/rust/rand_chacha/v0_3/lib/librand_chacha_lib.rlib
    [4/17] RUST .../rand_chacha/v0_2/lib/librand_chacha_lib.rlib
    [5/17] RUST .../rand_chacha/v0_3/lib/librand_chacha_lib.rlib

Both rlibs build and the six `usage of an unsafe block` errors are gone. The
rlibs were DELETED before that run, so it is a compile and not ninja reporting
it had nothing to do — an up-to-date output and a correct one look identical.

The config survives vendoring: the re-run left `gnrt_config.toml` byte-identical
to what it was before, and `third_party/rust/rand_chacha/v0_{2,3}/BUILD.gn` still
read `allow_unsafe = true`, unchanged. Byte-identical to the file as it stood,
not to what this patch alone produces — the first vendoring of a fresh tree also
auto-fills `allow_unsafe` for every crate the adblock closure adds that has no
key yet. Those entries are gnrt's own; what is verified here is that none of them
overwrites or displaces this one.

The target count is the vacuity floor: a `gn gen` that fails writes no build
graph, and everything downstream of one reads clean against nothing.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX

`//chrome/browser/oxy/adblock/rs:adblock_engine_ffi` still does not link, for a
reason no patch in this wave touches: rmp-serde 0.15.5 against rmp 0.8.15. It is
characterised in 066, which is the patch that declares rmp.

diff --git a/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml b/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
--- a/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
+++ b/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
@@ -557,6 +557,16 @@ allow_unsafe = false
 [crate.quote.extra_kv]
 allow_unsafe = false
 
+# Astro: this value is auto-filled by `fill_allow_unsafe_settings`
+# (tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs) and it guesses wrong here. Both vendored
+# epochs hold their single `unsafe` block inside the body of
+# `macro_rules! chacha_impl`, and `unsafe_code_detector` parses with `syn`,
+# which never descends into `syn::Macro::tokens`. Its own doc comment names
+# this miss and says a build failure is how it gets corrected. The audit
+# backing the value is in the Astro patch that sets it.
+[crate.rand_chacha]
+extra_kv = { allow_unsafe = true }
+
 [crate.read-fonts]
 extra_src_roots = ['../generated']
 extra_kv = { allow_unsafe = false }
